  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rational\n   We employ a Bayesian optimization framework to minimize the maximum overlap integral, combining an initial tight bound constraint and adaptive resolution switching. This method retrieves and iteratively refines solutions across multiple resolutions, leveraging interpolation to transfer promising candidates upward. The central idea is to balance exploration within fixed resolutions with resolution-based jumps to potentially break local optima. We address both convex and non-convex regions efficiently by adaptively calibrating correlation thresholds and using directed randomization alongside centralized repair mechanisms.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   Given domain [0,2] and target integral 1, the step function h(x) is approximated by n-point samples. For a candidate `h`, the objective is:\n      C5 = max_k [dx * (\u03c1_{n}(h, 1-h)[k])]\n   where \u03c1_{n} is full cross-correlation over the sampled domain. Constraints are 0<=h[i]<=1 and \u2211h[i]*dx=1.\n\n   Key operations:\n      - Repair: Enforces \u2211h[i]*dx=1 by clipping and scaling.\n      - Correlation: Uses FFT-based `np.correlate` mode=\"full\", with O(n log n) scaling.\n\n   Support: `scipy.optimize.gp_minimize` for Bayesian sampling, `cvxpy` for initial validation.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   ```python\n   def repair_h(x_vec, n_points, dx):\n       h_clipped = np.clip(x_vec, 0, 1)\n       current_sum = np.sum(h_clipped)\n       targets = n_points/2\n       best_targets = targets\n       tol = 1e-5\n       try:\n           if abs(current_sum - targets) < tol:\n               repaired = h_clipped\n           else:\n               rep_scale = best_targets / current_sum\n               h_scaled = h_clipped * rep_scale\n               h_scaled_clipped = np.clip(h_scaled, 0, 1)\n               h_repaired = h_scaled_clipped\n               sum_adjusted = np.sum(h_repaired)\n               if abs(sum_adjusted - best_targets) < tol:\n                   repaired = h_repaired\n               else:\n                   # Uniform fallback\n                   h_uniform = np.ones(len(x_vec)) * (best_targets/len(x_vec))\n                   repaired = h_uniform\n       except Exception as e:\n           repaired = np.ones(len(x_vec)) * (best_targets/len(x_vec))\n       return repaired\n\n   def compute_objective(h_vec, n, dx):\n       repair_obj = repair_h(h_vec, n, dx)\n       combined = 1 - repair_obj\n       corr = np.correlate(repair_obj, combined, mode=\"full\")\n       max_corr_val = np.max(corr)\n       return dx * max_corr_val\n\n   def run_phase_equivalent_fix(budget, n_start):\n       n = n_start\n       dx = 2.0 / n\n       target_sum = n/2\n       bounds = [(0.0, 1.0)] * n\n       space = list(bounds)\n\n       def objective(x):\n           return compute_objective(x, n, dx)\n\n       if n == 45 and initial_h_values is not None:\n           initial = initial_h_values.tolist()\n       else:\n           initial = [0.5] * n\n\n       from scipy.optimize import gp_minimize\n       res = gp_minimize(objective, space, \n                         n_calls=budget//10, \n                         initial_point=initial,\n                         n_iterations=budget//20,\n                         verbose=False)\n       best_eval = res.fun\n       best_point = res.x\n       return best_eval, best_point, n, dx\n\n   def interpolate_up(old_vec, old_n_new, new_n):\n       if new_n == old_n_new:\n           return old_vec, old_n_new\n       linear_t = np.linspace(0, 2, num=new_n)\n       old_base = np.linspace(0, 2, num=old_n_new)\n       f_interpolate = interp1d(old_base, old_vec, bounds_error=True, fill_value=\"extrapolate\")\n       new_vec = f_interpolate(linear_t)\n       return new_vec, new_n\n\n   def run_phases(time_budget):\n       t0 = time.time()\n       best_score = 0.381\n       best_vec = None\n       best_n = None\n       n_base = 45\n\n       score, candidate, n, dx = run_phase_equivalent_fix(time_budget//2, n_base)\n       if score < best_score:\n           best_score = score\n           best_vec = candidate\n           best_n = n\n\n       time_1 = time.time()\n       remaining_time = time_budget - time_1\n\n       if remaining_time > 0 and best_score < 0.381:\n           new_n = n_base * 2\n           new_vec, n_next = interpolate_up(candidate, n_base, new_n)\n           score2, candidate_next, n_next, dx_next = run_phase_equivalent_fix(remaining_time//2, n_next)\n           if score2 < best_score:\n               best_score = score2\n               best_vec = candidate_next\n               best_n = n_next\n\n       return best_vec, best_score, best_n, None\n\n   def double_resolution_if_possible(best_vec, best_n):\n       if best_n % 2 == 0:\n           try:\n               new_n = best_n * 2\n               new_vec, new_n = interpolate_up(best_vec, best_n//2, new_n)\n           except Exception as e:\n               return best_vec, best_n, None\n           else:\n               return new_vec, new_n, dx_next\n       return best_vec, best_n, None\n   ```\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Initial Resolution: 45 (parent's n)\n   - Bayesian Budget: 50 evaluations per phase\n   - Resolution Jumps: Only powers of two (45 -> 90 -> 180)\n   - Repair Fallback: Uniform initialization upon repair failure\n   - Interpolation: Linear between domain points\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Dominant cost: Each evaluation O(n log n) for correlation\n   - Total evaluations: 50 per phase * number of phases (max 3)\n   - 45 points: 50*45*log45 ~ 10,000 ops\n   - 90 points: 50*90*log90 ~ 40,000 ops\n   - Tolerance for missing improvement: If no improvement for two phases, switch resolution only if within 2% reduction.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n  - Compute objective on final vector: \n        `final_score = compute_objective(best_vec, best_n, dx_next if provided else 2/best_n)`\n  - Assert: `0 <= best_vec[i] <= 1` for all i\n  - Assert: `abs(sum(best_vec)/best_n - 0.5) < 1e-3` (numeric tolerance)\n  - Return `(best_vec, final_score, best_n, dx_next if available else 2/best_n)`",
